# Chrome DevTools MCP

Live DOM / focus / accessibility inspection via [`chrome-devtools-mcp`](https://github.com/ChromeDevTools/chrome-devtools-mcp), attached to the WSL-installed Google Chrome. Use when a UI bug (focus management, ARIA, etc.) can't be diagnosed from source alone.

Running Chrome inside WSL (instead of bridging to the Windows install) keeps the whole debugging flow on one side of the WSL↔Windows boundary — no port-forwarding or host-IP resolution. The WSL Chrome profile is isolated from any Windows browser and is signed into the test tenant only.

## One-time setup
- `google-chrome` installed in WSL (Ubuntu `.deb` from Google is fine).
- The MCP server is declared in `~/.copilot/mcp-config.json` (managed by chezmoi); no manual registration needed.

## Per-session
1. Launch Chrome (idempotent — no-op if the debug port is already answering):
   ```bash
   chrome-debug
   ```
   Script at `~/.local/bin/chrome-debug` runs `google-chrome --remote-debugging-port=9222 --user-data-dir=~/.cache/chrome-debug-profile`. WSLg surfaces the window in Windows automatically.
2. Sign into the test tenant if prompted — credentials live in Bitwarden.
3. Navigate to the page that exhibits the bug being diagnosed.
4. Tell the assistant "Chrome is ready" — it connects via the MCP at `http://127.0.0.1:9222`.

## Safety
- **Test tenant only.** Never sign into the WSL Chrome with your real work account.
- Close Chrome when the debugging session ends so the debugging port isn't left open.
- Review anything that looks like it's executing JS against sensitive state.
- Browser state stays in `~/.cache/chrome-debug-profile`, isolated from any other browser profile.

## Iframe debugging
- `press_key` via CDP doesn't reliably deliver Tab (and likely other keys) into focused iframe content. For keyboard-behavior tests, dispatch a synthetic `KeyboardEvent` via `evaluate_script`, or have the user drive the keyboard directly.
- Cross-origin iframes (e.g. Teams hosting Neptune): parent-document access to `iframe.contentDocument` is blocked. Use element UIDs from `take_snapshot` instead — they work across frame boundaries when passed as `args` to `evaluate_script`, and `document.activeElement` inside the function resolves to the element's own frame.

# Repo & Worktree Layout

All git repositories and their worktrees live under `~/repos/`. This layout is fixed —
apply it no matter which directory a session is launched from (including from inside a repo).

- **Canonical clones:** `~/repos/<repo>` (e.g. `~/repos/teams-client-workflows`). These stay on
  their default branch and are never edited directly.
- **Worktrees:** `~/repos/worktrees/<repo>/<branch-slug>`
  (e.g. `~/repos/worktrees/teams-client-workflows/5443403-tiny-fluentui-chunks`).

Rules:
- ALWAYS create worktrees at `~/repos/worktrees/<repo>/<branch-slug>`. **NEVER inside the repo
  directory** (no `.agent-wt/`, no repo subdir) and never elsewhere in `$HOME` or `~/.cache`.
  `<branch-slug>` is the branch name's final segment (the `<workitem-id>-<short-description>` part).
- The sandbox grants read/write to `~/repos`, so git and agent commands run in worktrees (which
  share the canonical repo's object store) without approval prompts.
- Create with:
  ```bash
  git -C ~/repos/<repo> worktree add ~/repos/worktrees/<repo>/<slug> -b <branchPrefix><slug>
  ```
- Remove a finished worktree with `git -C ~/repos/<repo> worktree remove <path>` — don't `rm -rf`
  the directory, which leaves a dangling registration needing `git worktree prune`.

# Docker Sandbox Delegation

After host-side framing and agreement on the next bounded step, default
substantial repository implementation, runtime experiments, and dependency
execution to Docker Sandbox using the `sandbox-work` skill. Do not wait for an
explicit sandbox request. Honor an explicit request to work locally.
The host gathers context and reviews results; a mountless VM runs repository
work or experiments. The `sbx-host` bridge exposes VM lifecycle, commands,
file transfer, and constrained private-package restore, not a prescribed workflow.
Follow the skill's discussion checkpoint before delegation; choosing isolation
does not establish the task's scope or approve an implementation.

- Keep external MCP access, Git-remote credentials, integration, and publishing on the host.
- Read-only host repository inspection for task framing and revision selection is
  allowed. Hand off before installing dependencies or running project tests/builds;
  missing host tooling is not a reason to provision a local implementation environment.
- Small documentation/configuration edits may stay local. Keep discussion on the
  host; once scope is agreed, proceed with delegation without another approval
  checkpoint unless new uncertainty changes the agreed step.
- Use ordinary Linux Git and `~/repos/.sbx-transfer/` to exchange committed history
  or results. Follow the repository/worktree layout above; never use Windows Git.
- Private Azure Artifacts credentials belong only in the bridge's restore process,
  not prompts, files, or general commands.
- Do not invoke `sbx.exe` from the command sandbox or grant `/init` or `/run/WSL`;
  that would expose arbitrary Windows process launch. Use the bridge instead.
